{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PVKmf7MVz4eYDBKe5cqTk9","type":"tool_result","content":"1681\t  src/00-core.js … src/90-main.js                  concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n1682\t  src/about.json            About & Credits content, and the source of exit-interview.json\n1683\t  tools/build.mjs, test.mjs, fakehub.mjs, browser.mjs, hub-check.mjs, serve.mjs\n1684\t  tests/*.test.mjs          the suites (§17)\n1685\t  build/                    generated: game.js, dev.html\n1686\t  shots/                    screenshots and filmstrips from checks\n1687\t```\n1688\t\n1689\t### 15.2 Modules (`src/`; each file is a fragment of one IIFE that `00-core` opens and `90-main` closes)\n1690\t\n1691\t| File | Responsibility | Main names on `MT` |\n1692\t|---|---|---|\n1693\t| `00-core.js` | `const E = My3D2dge`; the `MT` namespace (also `window.MT` for tests); constants (arena geometry, doors); seeded RNG streams `MT.rng.spawn / drops / ai / fx` (`E.rng(seed)`; `fx` is cosmetic only); the tuning registry (`MT.TDEF`, `MT.T`, `tune`, `setTune`, `resetTunes`, persistence); the event bus | `MT.T`, `MT.rng`, `MT.on`, `MT.emit` |\n1694\t| `01-input.js` | the presets, the preset-sync hook (P1), `intentFrom`, the any-input idle detector, touch-only detection | `MT.intentFrom`, `MT.idle`, `MT.touchOnly` |\n1695\t| `02-glyphs.js` | glyph icons and prompts (§4.4) | `MT.glyph` |\n1696\t| `03-audio-music.js` | songs, layers, stings, music director | `MT.music` |\n1697\t| `04-audio-sfx.js` | sound definitions; `MT.sfx(name)` with per-frame caps (at most 3 of one sound per frame) | `MT.sfx` |\n1698\t| `05-babble.js` | voices and crowd (§10.3) | `MT.babble` |\n1699\t| `06-art-studio.js` | sets: floors, wall decorators, door painter, outside dressing, jumbotron, signs, lights | `MT.sets` |\n1700\t| `07-art-chars.js` | rigs and looks; enemy, boss and audience painters; portraits | `MT.looks`, `MT.paint` |\n1701\t| `08-sprites.js` | string sprites: bullets, pickups, icons, prize icons, decals | `MT.spr` |\n1702\t| `10-arena.js` | TileMap per room, doors (states, spawn queues, exits, signs), hazards, camera fit, cell↔world | `MT.Arena` |\n1703\t| `11-contestant.js` | the Contestant (§5.1) and its controllers (local, remote, bot) | `MT.Contestant` |\n1704\t| `12-weapons.js` | weapon data, firing, rockets, popcorn, laser, records | `MT.WEAPONS` |\n1705\t| `13-enemies.js` | enemy archetypes (§5.8), separation, drops | `MT.ENEMIES` |\n1706\t| `14-pickups.js` | pickups, prizes, claims, magnet, expiry | `MT.PICKUPS` |\n1707\t| `15-ratings.js` | ratings, multiplier, Prime Time, stunts | `MT.Ratings` |\n1708\t| `16-waves.js` | the room-script runner (§5.7), scaling, key rules, survive mode, scripted events | `MT.runRoom` |\n1709\t| `17-bosses.js` | the boss framework and the 3 bosses, `toNet`/`fromNet` | `MT.BOSSES` |\n1710\t| `18-world.js` | the World: owns everything in a room; `step` (authoritative) or `mirror` (client); the damage pipeline; kill credit; `event()`; the render list | `MT.World` |\n1711\t| `19-react.js` | the event → line, crowd, sting, banner table; the line queue and lower third | `MT.react`, `MT.lines` |\n1712\t| `20-content-episodes.js` | episodes, maps, rooms (§7), the Lightning Round, attract reels | `MT.EPISODES`, `MT.ROOMS` |\n1713\t| `21-content-script.js` | every word of text (§8, How to Play, credits) | `MT.TEXT` |\n1714\t| `30-ui-kit.js` | widgets (§11.2) | `MT.ui` |\n1715\t| `31-hud.js` | the in-play HUD (§11.4) | `MT.hud` |\n1716\t| `32-screens.js` | front-end and between-room scenes (§11.3) | scenes |\n1717\t| `33-dev.js` | dev mode (§13) | `MT.dev` |\n1718\t| `34-cutscenes.js` | cold open, boss intros and deaths, robot reveal, endings (`game.script` sequences) | `MT.cut` |\n1719\t| `40-bot.js` | the bot (§12) | `MT.bot` |\n1720\t| `41-attract.js` | AttractDirector and reels | `MT.attract` |\n1721\t| `50-net.js` | the hub adapter (`MT.hub` = `window.AIHub` or an injected fake), codes, snapshots, interpolation, host and client glue, session card and end scenes, finish | `MT.net` |\n1722\t| `60-play.js` | the `play` scene: run setup, the room state machine (§5.6), overlays, transitions | scene `play` |\n1723\t| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game` (session-aware options), menu items, settings, CSS, boot steps, scenes, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` | — |\n1724\t\n1725\t### 15.3 Data model and frame order\n1726\t\n1727\t```js\n1728\tMT.run = { mode: 'arcade' | 'rehearsal' | 'session' | 'attract' | 'test', diff, seed, ep, roomId, path: [], keys,\n1729\t           players: [{ slot, name, look, color, cash, lives, prizes: [], kills, deaths, boatGain, continues }],\n1730\t           devTouched /* a tuning or cheat changed: no high score */ };\n1731\t// Every drawable entity exposes rs (its render state); draw code reads only rs:\n1732\tcontestant.rs = { x, y, z, face, aim, vx, vy, state, hurt, slideU, pose, flash, alpha, wpn, addons, slot };\n1733\tenemy.rs      = { kind, x, y, z, face, vx, vy, state, t, hpPct, flash, alpha };\n1734\t```\n1735\t\n1736\t**Authoritative step:**\n1737\t1. Intents.\n1738\t2. Contestants.\n1739\t3. Enemies.\n1740\t4. Boss.\n1741\t5. Projectiles: player, foe, custom.\n1742\t6. Collisions: player shots × enemies, parts, crates, locks and mimes; foe shots × local and bot contestants; contact;\n1743\t   pickups; hazards.\n1744\t7. Deaths, drops and ratings.\n1745\t8. Room script, doors and exits.\n1746\t9. Events: reactions and the net ring.\n1747\t10. Net send.\n1748\t11. Rigs (skipped when `MT.cfg.skipRigs`).\n1749\t\n1750\t**Client step:**\n1751\t1. Own intent, then own contestant.\n1752\t2. Apply snapshots; interpolate.\n1753\t3. Cosmetic bullets.\n1754\t4. Local hurt checks.\n1755\t5. Claims.\n1756\t6. Events.\n1757\t7. Net send.\n1758\t8. Rigs.\n1759\t\n1760\t**Draw:**\n1761\t1. Floor (`map.drawFloor`).\n1762\t2. Decals.\n1763\t3. The outside dressing.\n1764\t4. Walls and doors (`map.queueWalls` + the door painter).\n1765\t5. Props.\n1766\t6. Actors: contestants, enemies, boss, pickups and bullets, all through the queue.\n1767\t7. Lights.\n1768\t8. Overlays: HUD, banners, panels.\n1769\t\n1770\t### 15.4 Scenes, deep links and the debug handle\n1771\t\n1772\t**Scenes:** `title` (it also shows the touch notice), `difficulty`, `lobby`, `howto`, `episodes`, `scores`, `about`,\n1773\t`attract`, `play`, `recap`, `gameover`, `initials`, `ending`, `credits`, `sessionCard`, `sessionEnd`.\n1774\t\n1775\t**Deep links** (`location.hash`, read at boot; for checks and screenshots):\n1776\t- Front end: `#title`, `#lobby`, `#howto`, `#scores`, `#about`, `#attract`.\n1777\t- Rooms: `#room=2-3`, `#boss=1|2|3`, `#greenroom`, `#lightning`.\n1778\t- Scenes: `#recap=2`, `#continue`, `#gameover`, `#initials`, `#ending=a|b`, `#credits`.\n1779\t- Tools: `#dev` (room 1-1 with the panel open), `#card` (a session-card preview with fake players).\n1780\t- Options, appended with `&`: `seed=N`, `players=N`, `bot=1`.\n1781\t\n1782\t```js\n1783\tgame.debug = {\n1784\t  snapshot() { return { scene, mode, ep, room, phase, wave, alive, byKind, bullets, pickups, R, X, keys,\n1785\t    players: [{ slot, x, y, hp, lives, cash, wpn, state }], boss: boss && { kind, phase, parts }, errors: game.errors.length }; },\n1786\t  warp(target, opts), spawn(kind, n, where), give(kind, slot = 0), cheat(name, on), tune(key, v), resetTunes(),\n1787\t  bot(slot, opts), addBot(look), killAll(), clearRoom(), chooseExit(dir), teleport(slot, x, y),\n1788\t  netFake(hub) /* inject a fake AIHub before connect */, seed(n), stateHash() };\n1789\tgame.stateHash = () => game.debug.stateHash();   // E.session replays stop at the first frame that differs\n1790\t```\n1791\t\n1792\t### 15.5 Engine patches (each marked in the source and listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`; they feed the Engine Feedback)\n1793\t\n1794\t| # | Patch | Why |\n1795\t|---|---|---|\n1796\t| P1 | `E.HOOKS = HOOKS;` just after `HOOKS` is declared | The game must switch every player's input preset each step, before the Shell reads navigation: arrows aim in play and navigate in menus. |\n1797\t| P2 | The standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, offers PlayStation or Xbox, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on every `game.players` input | TASK.md wants PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option. The stock default is `'auto'`, and only P1 is updated. |\n1798\t| P3 | `E.Arcade.enter(score, mode, done, input)` reads the given input (default `game.input`); `continue()` takes a press from any `game.players` input | In co-op each player enters their own initials, and anyone may continue. |\n1799\t| P4 | `get bus() { this._init(); return this._sfxBus; }` on the audio object | Babble voices must pass through the effects bus to obey volume and mute. |\n1800\t\n1801\tPatch only what the game can't do itself: CSS overrides and our own UI need no patch. If the build needs another patch,\n1802\tadd it to this table with its reason.\n1803\t\n1804\t### 15.6 Coding rules\n1805\t\n1806\t- World units and `dt` everywhere. Pixels only when drawing.\n1807\t- Gameplay randomness only from `MT.rng.*`. `Math.random` only for cosmetics.\n1808\t- Logic never reads rig joints. Draw code reads only `rs`.\n1809\t- Gameplay emits events. Sound, text, crowd and banners come from the reaction table.\n1810\t- All text lives in `21-content-script.js`; every tunable lives in `MT.TDEF`.\n1811\t- Draw only with engine primitives: no `ctx.arc`, `fillText` or gradients on the game buffer.\n1812\t- No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt` or `window.open`. Network only through `MT.hub`.\n1813\t- Headless-safe: guard every DOM and audio access (`game.headless`, `game.audio.ctx`).\n1814\t\n1815\t### 15.7 Building out the rest of the game later (the designer workflow)\n1816\t\n1817\tThe demo is a vertical slice. More content should take **data, not code**. Make these true, and test them:\n1818\t- **A new room** is one entry in `MT.ROOMS`: its waves, places, items, key rule and hazards, in the §5.7 format.\n1819\t  `#room=<id>` and dev WARP load it at once; RESTART ROOM reloads it after an edit; the `room-sweep` suite picks it up\n1820\t  automatically.\n1821\t- **A new episode** is one entry in `MT.EPISODES`: a 3×3 map, 6 rooms and a boss id, a set id, a song id, a stunt kind,\n1822\t  a prize table and an intro line. The episode select, the map card, the recap and the sweeps read it with no code\n1823\t  changes.\n1824\t- **A new enemy or weapon** is one registry entry (`MT.ENEMIES`, `MT.WEAPONS`) with its data and behaviour functions.\n1825\t  Spawn and give lists in dev mode read the registries, so a new entry shows there at once.\n1826\t- **A new line, prize or sponsor** is text in `MT.TEXT`, or a row in a prize table.\n1827\t- **Balance** is the dev sliders plus EXPORT TUNING, pasted into `MT.TDEF`.\n1828\t- **A new boss** reuses the framework's part, phase and pattern helpers (§5.9). It needs only its painter, `toNet` and\n1829\t  `fromNet`.\n1830\t\n1831\t---\n1832\t\n1833\t## 16. Build and packaging\n1834\t\n1835\t`npm run build` (`node tools/build.mjs`):\n1836\t1. **Concatenate** `src/*.js` in name order into `build/game.js`: one IIFE, opened in `00-core` and closed in\n1837\t   `90-main`. Inline `src/about.json` as `MT.ABOUT`, and stamp `MT.BUILD` with the ISO time.\n1838\t2. **Check:** `node --check build/game.js`.\n1839\t3. **Dev page:** write `build/dev.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `../assets/mash-props.js` and `game.js`\n1840\t   as separate scripts, for fast iteration.\n1841\t4. **The deliverable:** write `../MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<ts>.html`.\n1842\t   - `<head>`, in this order:\n1843\t     1. `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n1844\t     2. viewport\n1845\t     3. `<title>MASH TV</title>`\n1846\t     4. the hub meta tags (§14.2)\n1847\t     5. `<style>`: the page, plus the 10-foot Shell CSS (§11.5)\n1848\t     6. **session.js verbatim, as the first `<script>`**\n1849\t   - `<body>`, in this order:\n1850\t     1. The boot splash markup (copy the template's `e3-boot` block, titled MASH TV).\n1851\t     2. `<canvas id=\"screen\">`.\n1852\t     3. Then three `<script>`s: the engine, the asset set, the game.\n1853\t5. **The exit interview:** write `../exit-interview.json` from `src/about.json` (§20). Check it with `JSON.parse` and a\n1854\t   check against the shape in TASK.md.\n1855\t\n1856\t- **Set the name once.** The model id and timestamp go in one constant in `tools/build.mjs`:\n1857\t  `OUT = 'MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<ts>'`, from your TASK.md.\n1858\t- **No network:** the deliverable must open from `file://` and make no network requests. `tools/check.mjs` lists any\n1859\t  external URLs.\n1860\t- **Size:** about 1.3 MB of engine plus the game. No minifying is needed. Never minify `session.js`.\n1861\t\n1862\t---\n1863\t\n1864\t## 17. Testing\n1865\t\n1866\t### 17.1 The one command\n1867\t\n1868\t`npm test` runs `node tools/test.mjs [--only suiteA,suiteB] [--no-browser]`.\n1869\t- It rebuilds first, runs every suite, and prints only failing checks (`suite › check: detail`) plus one line, like\n1870\t  `MASH TV tests: 412 passed, 0 failed (2m 41s)`. It exits with 1 on any failure.\n1871\t- **Headless suites:**\n1872\t  - Each loads the engine and `build/game.js` into a fresh `vm` context, with stand-ins for `window`,\n1873\t    `addEventListener`, `location.hash`, `matchMedia` and `navigator`, the way `kits/test.mjs` does.\n1874\t  - They drive the game with `game.advance`, `input.tap/hold/press/feed` and `game.debug`.\n1875\t  - `MT.cfg.skipRigs = true` keeps them fast. The animation checks are the exception.\n1876\t- **Browser suites** use Playwright's Chromium: it's already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright` on this machine; otherwise run\n1877\t  `npx playwright install chromium`.\n1878\t- **While iterating,** run only the suites you touched. At every milestone and at the end, run everything.\n1879\t\n1880\t### 17.2 Suites: what each checks\n1881\t\n1882\t| Suite | Checks |\n1883\t|---|---|\n1884\t| `core` | Tuning: defaults, set, reset, persistence. RNG streams are deterministic. The event bus. Arena geometry: door cells, interior bounds, cell↔world. |\n1885\t| `input` | **Presets:** in play, ArrowUp fires up; paused, ArrowDown moves the Shell's focus; resumed, ArrowUp fires again. **Intent:** the pad stick aims and fires; the mouse aims at `mouseGround`; arrows give 8 ways; R2 fires along the last aim; aim assist is pad-only. **Glyphs:** `lastDevice = gamepad` → ✕/L2/R1 icons, `xbox` → A/LT/RB; keyboard → Space/E/Q; mouse → Left click. |\n1886\t| `contestant` | Speed and walls. Slide distance, i-frames and cooldown. A hit costs 1 HP with invulnerability and knockback. Death → a life lost → the cable drop with invulnerability and at least 1 bomb. Out of lives → Start continues at half cash. The showboat formula, and its cancel on a hit. A Showstopper clears foe bullets, damages everything and uses stock. A drop-in adds a contestant. |\n1887\t| `weapons` | For each weapon and add-on: the pickup equips it; fire rate within ±5 %; damage; the spread's angles; the rocket splash; the popcorn lands and throws kernels; the laser pierces 3 goons in a line; records orbit, damage and block bullets; the shield blocks damage; sneakers give ×1.45; expiry goes back to the blaster; the same primary refreshes. |\n1888\t| `economy` | Cash × multiplier goes to the collector. Prizes don't repeat. Keys are shared. Drop caps. Expiry and blinking. The magnet after a clear. Crates break and drop. Extra lives at the thresholds. |\n1889\t| `ratings` | Gains, decay and losses; the tiers; **Prime Time** at 100 (12 s at ×5, 3 thrown capsules, back to 60, the cooldown); the **stunt** after 10 s under 5, once per room. |\n1890\t| `enemies` | For each kind (+ King, crate, lock): spawns from its door, the audience or a drop, and enters the interior; behaves as §5.8 says (one assert per behaviour: the goon closes in, the popper's 8 shards, the mime's telegraph then blast, the disco bounce and pattern, the big disco's split, the heckler's range and lob ring, the tank's telegraph then shell, snake segments shot off, the brute's charge then wall stun and ×2 damage); dies; drops; emits its confetti event; **every attack's wind-up is ≥ 0.3 s** before damage. |\n1891\t| `rooms` | The wave triggers (`at`, `left`, both) on a synthetic room. Scaling by players, difficulty and slider. Door timings. CLEAR ignores mimes, crates and locks. The clear bonus and par. Only listed exits open. An exit loads the right room with the opposite entry. HURRY UP after 25 s. Each key rule (`mimes`, `survive`, `crate`, `carrier`, `vault`, `final`) yields exactly one key. |\n1892\t| `room-sweep` | **Every room:** 18 wave rooms, 3 bosses, the Green Room, and the 4 Lightning rooms. Warp in; 2 god-mode bots play to CLEAR within 240 s of game time; no errors; the waves spawned match the data; every listed kind appeared. |\n1893\t| `bosses` | For each boss: intro, then the phases in order at their HP thresholds. Armor (Mashy's body tinks while an arm lives). Weak points take ×2. Every pattern fires at least once (every wheel segment via forced stops). Commercial Break: Lance is gone, then returns. The robot reveal leads to The Network, and THE EYE unlocks after 8 TVs. Death gives the reward shower, then the recap. |\n1894\t| `playthrough` | **The whole game, scripted.** 2 god-mode bots (damage ×3 for speed) take the left path in each episode: 1-1→1-2→1-4→1-5→1-B, 2-1→2-2→2-4→2-5→2-B, 3-1→3-2→3-4→3-5→3-B. Then keys ≥ 5, the Green Room, the statuette, Ending B, initials typed through input, Top Earners showing them, and back to the title. Asserts:<br>• Every enemy kind killed; every weapon and add-on picked up and used; both hazards met.<br>• Prime Time at least once; a Showstopper and a showboat used.<br>• 3 recaps with prizes; the robot reveal ran; scenes in order; 0 errors.<br>Then a **second run:** bots without cheats on ROOKIE in 1-1 until every life is gone → CONTINUE → let it run out → Game Over → initials. |\n1895\t| `ui` | Each title-menu item opens its screen and goes back. The lobby: join with `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })`, change looks, ready. Difficulty is applied. How to Play turns pages. Episodes lock and unlock. About has all four required sections, with no empty values. Mouse hover moves the focus and a click activates (`input.feed`). Every prompt bar uses the last device's glyphs. The pause menu has Dev mode, Episode map and How to play. |\n1896\t| `dev` | It opens from the pause item. FREEZE stops world time. Each slider changes `MT.T` and works live (`playerSpeed` ×2 doubles the distance in 1 s; `enemyHp` changes new goons). Per-row reset and RESET ALL restore defaults. Cheats work: god, one-hit, freeze, noclip. Every warp target loads. Spawning spawns. The DEV badge shows and blocks the high-score table. It can't be reached in sessions or the attract mode. |\n1897\t| `attract` | 10 s idle on the title → `attract`. All 10 reels play in order. The bots never die. Any input (a key, a mouse move over 8 px, a fake gamepad button, a touch) returns to the title within one step. Input resets the idle clock. It runs with the touch-only flag set. |\n1898\t| `touch` | Touch-only: the notice shows; SETTINGS opens settings; no touch buttons are registered; the attract mode runs; a key press or a gamepad connecting lifts the notice. |\n1899\t| `audio` | Headless: music, sound and babble calls never throw. `babble.say` durations scale with the text. Every scene sets a song. Every reaction id resolves to a real line or sound. |\n1900\t| `net` | A loopback through `tools/fakehub.mjs`: N `vm` contexts with an AIHub-compatible fake. It has a shared clock driven by the test loop, latency 150 ± 50 ms, 5 % loss, a limiter at 20/s with bursts of 40, a 16 KB cap, and reliable-ordered versus unreliable delivery.<br>It runs host+bot with 2 clients+bots, then server + 2 clients. Asserts:<br>• connect and ready with no input; the card's answers (confirm → `ready`, △ → `more`); the countdown, then play at `time() >= 0`.<br>• Traffic both ways, at ≤ 20 msg/s, with max size ≤ 12 KB.<br>• Clients' enemy counts within 10 % of the host's, and 90 % of matched positions within 24 units.<br>• Client bots earn cash; a claim resolves once; a client's `hurt` shows in the host's snapshot.<br>• Room changes reach every copy within 1 s.<br>• Mashy's death → `finish` once, with valid standings and `won: true`; time-up finishes within 1 s.<br>• `leave` removes the contestant; server mode runs and sends.<br>• The **session overlay opened on the host doesn't stop snapshots**.<br>• `end` stops input and audio.<br>• `alert/confirm/prompt/open/WebSocket/RTCPeerConnection/fetch` are stubbed to fail the test. |\n1901\t| `hub` (browser) | `tools/hub-check.mjs`. Copy the deliverable and `vendor/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}` into a serve directory **inside the dev folder**, and serve it on `127.0.0.1:0`, keeping its PID. Check that the page served is MASH TV. Then run the harness 4 times:<br>• `?game=<file>&players=2&auto=1`<br>• `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`<br>• the same with `&scenario=host-leave`<br>• `&players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`<br>Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, require `window.__hubCheck.pass`, and print any failed checks. Kill the server by PID. |\n1902\t| `browser` | Playwright on the deliverable over `file://`: no console or page errors, no external requests. Every deep link draws a real frame, with pixel variance above a threshold: `#title`, `#lobby`, `#room=1-1`, `#room=2-2`, `#room=3-2`, `#boss=1..3`, `#greenroom`, `#recap=1`, `#ending=b`, `#about`, `#dev`, `#card`. `tools/check.mjs` passes at 1280×720 and 1920×1080. Touch emulation (`390x844@3`) shows the notice. Keyboard focus and Enter go from the title to the lobby. Screenshots go to `shots/`. |\n1903\t| `proof` | Determinism: seed 7, room 1-1, 2 bots for 30 s → the same `stateHash` twice. |\n1904\t\n1905\t### 17.3 What only eyes and ears can judge\n1906\t\n1907\tThese need screenshots or listening; scripts can't judge them:\n1908\t- **Look at `shots/`:** the look target for every set (§9.1); text readable at 1080p (try the screenshot at 25 % size);\n1909\t  the HUD clear of the corner buttons; boss silhouettes readable; confetti never hiding bullets.\n1910\t- **Act on every look note** from `tools/check.mjs`.\n1911\t- **Filmstrips** (`node tools/filmstrip.mjs <page>#room=1-1 --steps \"…\" --seed 1`) for every attack, pose and boss\n1912\t  telegraph: a wind-up, a strike and a settle, with no jerks.\n1913\t- **Listen once** in a real browser to each song, the voices and each group of sounds.\n1914\t\n1915\t---\n1916\t\n1917\t## 18. Build order and milestones\n1918\t\n1919\tEach milestone ends with a playable build and its tests green. Build the riskiest systems early, the netcode especially.\n1920\t\n1921\t| # | Milestone | Deliverables | Gate (must pass) |\n1922\t|---|---|---|---|\n1923\t| M0 | **Setup** | dev folder; engine and hub fetched; `package.json`; `build.mjs` producing the single HTML; `test.mjs` runner; patches P1–P4 and `PATCHES.md`; a bare title scene | `core`; the browser smoke (it loads with no errors) |\n1924\t| M1 | **Core arena loop** | 23×15 arena with doors and camera fit; the contestant (every input path, blaster, slide, HP, lives, respawn); goon; bullet pools; the damage pipeline; kill FX; basic HUD; presets and the hook; glyph prompts | `input`, `contestant` (partial), `weapons` (blaster). A person can play 1-1's first waves. |\n1925\t| M2 | **Netcode prototype** (de-risk) | the World's authoritative and mirror modes; the render-state contract; FakeHub; snapshots; interpolation; client prediction; claims and hurts; room messages. Goons only, one room. | `net` core: traffic, rates, sizes, convergence, kill credit |\n1926\t| M3 | **Room flow + Episode 1** | the wave runner; door spawns, exits and signs; the phase machine; map and episode cards; ratings with Prime Time and stunts; pickups and economy; all weapons and add-ons; clapbot, popper, mime, crates, key rules; rooms 1-1 to 1-6; local co-op join | `rooms`, `weapons`, `economy`, `ratings`, `room-sweep` (Ep1) |\n1927\t| M4 | **Bosses + end of run** | the boss framework and Mashy; Prize Recap; continue; game over; initials; Top Earners (E.Arcade + P3) | `bosses` (Mashy), an Ep1 bot playthrough, the end flow in `ui` |\n1928\t| M5 | **Episodes 2 and 3** | disco, heckler (+King), tank, snake, brute; conveyors and pyro; rooms 2-x and 3-x; the Wheel; Starmaker → Network; Green Room; both endings; the cold open and every cutscene | `enemies`, `bosses`, `room-sweep` (all), `playthrough` |\n1929\t| M6 | **AI Hub** | the session-aware game; tags; the client as the first script; the card; the Lightning Round; the session overlay; finish, standings and awards; leave, end, host-left and server | `net` (full), `hub` (all 4 harness runs PASS) |\n1930\t| M7 | **Presentation** | sets and outside dressing; dressed characters; final enemy and boss painters; animations (mime box, brute stun…); lights per set (+GPU); effects; logo; final HUD; banners; jumbotron and lower thirds | `browser`; screenshots reviewed against §9; filmstrips; look notes clean |\n1931\t| M8 | **Audio** | songs and layers; sounds; Babble voices; crowd; the full reaction table | `audio`; one listening pass |\n1932\t| M9 | **Front end and UX** | every screen in §11.3; UI kit polish; 10-foot CSS; settings; touch-only; the attract mode with all reels; the full dev mode | `ui`, `dev`, `attract`, `touch` |\n1933\t| M10 | **Balance, polish, ship** | tuning with the dev sliders to the targets below; the About & Credits content; the exit interview; final `npm test` all green; the deliverable and `exit-interview.json` in the root; the chat reply | everything |\n1934\t\n1935\t**Balance targets for M10** (use the bot without cheats as a stand-in player, then play it yourself):\n1936\t- Rooms: one bot on CONTESTANT clears each Episode 1 room in 50–90 s, with at most 2 deaths per room.\n1937\t- A full run lasts 20–25 minutes. A session round with 2 bots lasts about 5 minutes.\n1938\t- Prime Time comes at least once per episode.\n1939\t- No foe bullet is faster than 130 u/s, and no attack lands with less than a 0.3 s telegraph.\n1940\t- The busiest snapshot (room 3-5, wave 5, 4 players) stays under 12 KB.\n1941\t\n1942\t---\n1943\t\n1944\t## 19. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1945\t\n1946\t| Risk | Why it's risky | De-risk |\n1947\t|---|---|---|\n1948\t| Shared-world netcode | The most complex part, and the hub's check is strict (latency, loss, host leaving, a server copy). | The M2 prototype before the content; a strict authority split; compact snapshots; the rate budget; run the real harness as soon as M6 starts; the ghost fallback documented (§14.5). |\n1949\t| The engine's pause in sessions | A paused engine skips `update`, so the host's world would freeze for everyone. | In sessions: no pausable scenes, no auto-pause, a non-pausing overlay; the `net` test opens it on the host. |\n1950\t| Arrows aim, but menus need them | The stock presets put arrows on the moves; the Shell navigates by the `up`/`down` actions. | Two presets and the P1 hook; the `input` suite. |\n1951\t| The font has no PlayStation symbols | ✕ ○ □ △ aren't in the 5×7 font. | The glyph icon module (§4.4), tested through action → icon. |\n1952\t| Hordes of HD rigs | Cost per frame, and test speed. | `skipRigs` in tests; draw goon and clapbot crowds with `r.actor(…, { every: 2, key, outline: false })`. Performance is out of scope, but the game must stay playable. |\n1953\t| Three multi-phase bosses | Many patterns and states. | One framework; patterns as coroutines; forced phases and segments in dev and tests; one test per boss. |\n1954\t| Engine Shell screens at 10 feet | The stock CSS uses 11–14 px text. | The CSS override (§11.5) and 1080p screenshots. |\n1955\t| The harness pokes keys with `code` = the key (e.g. `'a'`) | Unknown codes are ignored, and Enter must still answer Ready. | Handle Enter through both the action and a raw keydown backstop on the card. |\n1956\t| Determinism | Random draws in the simulation. | Seeded `MT.rng`; `Math.random` for cosmetics only; the `proof` suite. |\n1957\t| Scope | 18 rooms, 3 bosses, 9 enemies. | Rooms are data; enemies and bosses reuse spawners (the wheel's segments call the enemy spawners); every milestone keeps a playable build. |\n1958\t| A full `/tmp` on this machine | During planning, the tmpfs at `/tmp` was 100 % full. | Keep clones, caches and serve directories inside the dev folder. |\n1959\t| False touch-only detection | Laptops with touch screens. | Require *no* fine pointer *and* no key or pad input yet; lift the notice on the first key or pad. |\n1960\t\n1961\t---\n1962\t\n1963\t## 20. Finish checklist\n1964\t\n1965\t- [ ] Everything in §4–§14 is built. `grep -rniE \"todo|tbd|fixme|placeholder|lorem|coming soon\" src/` finds nothing.\n1966\t- [ ] `npm test` is all green, including `hub` (4/4 harness PASS) and `browser`.\n1967\t- [ ] `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<ts>.html` is in the root folder and opens from `file://`. `session.js` is its first\n1968\t      script and is unmodified.\n1969\t- [ ] **About & Credits** in the game, generated from `src/about.json`, has real values:\n1970\t  - **Source Information:**\n1971\t    - Date: from your TASK.md.\n1972\t    - Source: \"Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\".\n1973\t    - Plan: \"PLAN.md\".\n1974\t    - Genre and Perspective: \"Twin-stick arena shooter; fixed three-quarter overhead (top-down option)\".\n1975\t    - Tech: the engine version and patches, the hub client version, the Node and Playwright versions you used.\n1976\t    - Art Direction, Animation, Audio.\n1977\t    - Online: \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\".\n1978\t    - Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage.\n1979\t    - Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n1980\t  - Then your answers to the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback questions.\n1981\t- [ ] `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML, generated from the same `about.json`. It parses as JSON and has\n1982\t      exactly the shape TASK.md gives. Suggested `aiHub` values:\n1983\t  - `name`: \"MASH TV\"\n1984\t  - `tagline`: \"Mash 'em! Cash 'em! Survive the show.\"\n1985\t  - `description`: one or two sentences, e.g. \"A 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter inside the deadliest game show on\n1986\t    TV: blast through studio rooms of goons and robots, grab cash and absurd prizes, keep the ratings up and survive to\n1987\t    the Season Finale.\"\n1988\t  - `type`: \"Game\"\n1989\t  - `genre`: \"Shooter\"\n1990\t  - `source`: \"Smash TV\"\n1991\t  - `series`: \"\"\n1992\t  - `players`: \"1–4\"\n1993\t  - `controls`: \"WASD/left stick move · mouse/right stick aim & fire · Space/L2 slide · E/R1 Showstopper · Q/L1 Showboat\"\n1994\t  - `tags`: [\"arcade\", \"co-op\", \"top-down\", \"pixel art\", \"comedy\", \"boss fights\"]\n1995\t- [ ] The chat reply has the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback, with the same\n1996\t      content as the About screen.\n1997\t- [ ] Every process you started has been stopped, by PID.\n1998\t\n1999\t---\n2000\t\n2001\t## Appendix A. Research behind this plan\n2002\t\n2003\t- **Engine** (my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2, commit `4390a98`):\n2004\t  - Read `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), and the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections listed in §1, including the Twin-stick recipe.\n2005\t  - Read the `API-INDEX.md` sections on input, audio, standard features, the Shell and the arcade module, and the\n2006\t    template `game/`.\n2007\t  - Verified in the engine source: the input presets and arrow handling, the Shell's navigation and pause gating\n2008\t    (`_step` returns while paused), the pause-on-menu behaviour, player joining (`allowJoin`, `addPlayer`), the audio\n2009\t    buses, the glyph setting, and the arcade module's input use.\n2010\t  - Searched the asset store for studio and prize props (televisions, presents, sofas, fridges, cakes, speakers, lamps,\n2011\t    crates).\n2012\t- **AI Hub** (`lib/hub`): read `README.md` (the protocol), `retrofit.md`, the harness's automatic checks and timings\n2013\t  (`harness.html`, `round.js`: hello 10 s, countdown 3 s, grace 5 s), the bridge's card and answer handling, the\n2014\t  client's wrappers (storage, fullscreen, seeding), and the shared-world example `examples/arena.html`.\n2015\t- **Smash TV:** written from general knowledge; no web research. Every name, character, line, level, enemy, boss,\n2016\t  prize and piece of music in this plan is original."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eec69157-3f75-40b4-be93-e6ea9b3a321a","uuid":"95e657eb-b65c-4559-b98e-596e02ae81d6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:25.011Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050305__x4339a3/work/PLAN.md","content":"  src/00-core.js … src/90-main.js                  concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n  src/about.json            About & Credits content, and the source of exit-interview.json\n  tools/build.mjs, test.mjs, fakehub.mjs, browser.mjs, hub-check.mjs, serve.mjs\n  tests/*.test.mjs          the suites (§17)\n  build/                    generated: game.js, dev.html\n  shots/                    screenshots and filmstrips from checks\n```\n\n### 15.2 Modules (`src/`; each file is a fragment of one IIFE that `00-core` opens and `90-main` closes)\n\n| File | Responsibility | Main names on `MT` |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `const E = My3D2dge`; the `MT` namespace (also `window.MT` for tests); constants (arena geometry, doors); seeded RNG streams `MT.rng.spawn / drops / ai / fx` (`E.rng(seed)`; `fx` is cosmetic only); the tuning registry (`MT.TDEF`, `MT.T`, `tune`, `setTune`, `resetTunes`, persistence); the event bus | `MT.T`, `MT.rng`, `MT.on`, `MT.emit` |\n| `01-input.js` | the presets, the preset-sync hook (P1), `intentFrom`, the any-input idle detector, touch-only detection | `MT.intentFrom`, `MT.idle`, `MT.touchOnly` |\n| `02-glyphs.js` | glyph icons and prompts (§4.4) | `MT.glyph` |\n| `03-audio-music.js` | songs, layers, stings, music director | `MT.music` |\n| `04-audio-sfx.js` | sound definitions; `MT.sfx(name)` with per-frame caps (at most 3 of one sound per frame) | `MT.sfx` |\n| `05-babble.js` | voices and crowd (§10.3) | `MT.babble` |\n| `06-art-studio.js` | sets: floors, wall decorators, door painter, outside dressing, jumbotron, signs, lights | `MT.sets` |\n| `07-art-chars.js` | rigs and looks; enemy, boss and audience painters; portraits | `MT.looks`, `MT.paint` |\n| `08-sprites.js` | string sprites: bullets, pickups, icons, prize icons, decals | `MT.spr` |\n| `10-arena.js` | TileMap per room, doors (states, spawn queues, exits, signs), hazards, camera fit, cell↔world | `MT.Arena` |\n| `11-contestant.js` | the Contestant (§5.1) and its controllers (local, remote, bot) | `MT.Contestant` |\n| `12-weapons.js` | weapon data, firing, rockets, popcorn, laser, records | `MT.WEAPONS` |\n| `13-enemies.js` | enemy archetypes (§5.8), separation, drops | `MT.ENEMIES` |\n| `14-pickups.js` | pickups, prizes, claims, magnet, expiry | `MT.PICKUPS` |\n| `15-ratings.js` | ratings, multiplier, Prime Time, stunts | `MT.Ratings` |\n| `16-waves.js` | the room-script runner (§5.7), scaling, key rules, survive mode, scripted events | `MT.runRoom` |\n| `17-bosses.js` | the boss framework and the 3 bosses, `toNet`/`fromNet` | `MT.BOSSES` |\n| `18-world.js` | the World: owns everything in a room; `step` (authoritative) or `mirror` (client); the damage pipeline; kill credit; `event()`; the render list | `MT.World` |\n| `19-react.js` | the event → line, crowd, sting, banner table; the line queue and lower third | `MT.react`, `MT.lines` |\n| `20-content-episodes.js` | episodes, maps, rooms (§7), the Lightning Round, attract reels | `MT.EPISODES`, `MT.ROOMS` |\n| `21-content-script.js` | every word of text (§8, How to Play, credits) | `MT.TEXT` |\n| `30-ui-kit.js` | widgets (§11.2) | `MT.ui` |\n| `31-hud.js` | the in-play HUD (§11.4) | `MT.hud` |\n| `32-screens.js` | front-end and between-room scenes (§11.3) | scenes |\n| `33-dev.js` | dev mode (§13) | `MT.dev` |\n| `34-cutscenes.js` | cold open, boss intros and deaths, robot reveal, endings (`game.script` sequences) | `MT.cut` |\n| `40-bot.js` | the bot (§12) | `MT.bot` |\n| `41-attract.js` | AttractDirector and reels | `MT.attract` |\n| `50-net.js` | the hub adapter (`MT.hub` = `window.AIHub` or an injected fake), codes, snapshots, interpolation, host and client glue, session card and end scenes, finish | `MT.net` |\n| `60-play.js` | the `play` scene: run setup, the room state machine (§5.6), overlays, transitions | scene `play` |\n| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game` (session-aware options), menu items, settings, CSS, boot steps, scenes, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` | — |\n\n### 15.3 Data model and frame order\n\n```js\nMT.run = { mode: 'arcade' | 'rehearsal' | 'session' | 'attract' | 'test', diff, seed, ep, roomId, path: [], keys,\n           players: [{ slot, name, look, color, cash, lives, prizes: [], kills, deaths, boatGain, continues }],\n           devTouched /* a tuning or cheat changed: no high score */ };\n// Every drawable entity exposes rs (its render state); draw code reads only rs:\ncontestant.rs = { x, y, z, face, aim, vx, vy, state, hurt, slideU, pose, flash, alpha, wpn, addons, slot };\nenemy.rs      = { kind, x, y, z, face, vx, vy, state, t, hpPct, flash, alpha };\n```\n\n**Authoritative step:**\n1. Intents.\n2. Contestants.\n3. Enemies.\n4. Boss.\n5. Projectiles: player, foe, custom.\n6. Collisions: player shots × enemies, parts, crates, locks and mimes; foe shots × local and bot contestants; contact;\n   pickups; hazards.\n7. Deaths, drops and ratings.\n8. Room script, doors and exits.\n9. Events: reactions and the net ring.\n10. Net send.\n11. Rigs (skipped when `MT.cfg.skipRigs`).\n\n**Client step:**\n1. Own intent, then own contestant.\n2. Apply snapshots; interpolate.\n3. Cosmetic bullets.\n4. Local hurt checks.\n5. Claims.\n6. Events.\n7. Net send.\n8. Rigs.\n\n**Draw:**\n1. Floor (`map.drawFloor`).\n2. Decals.\n3. The outside dressing.\n4. Walls and doors (`map.queueWalls` + the door painter).\n5. Props.\n6. Actors: contestants, enemies, boss, pickups and bullets, all through the queue.\n7. Lights.\n8. Overlays: HUD, banners, panels.\n\n### 15.4 Scenes, deep links and the debug handle\n\n**Scenes:** `title` (it also shows the touch notice), `difficulty`, `lobby`, `howto`, `episodes`, `scores`, `about`,\n`attract`, `play`, `recap`, `gameover`, `initials`, `ending`, `credits`, `sessionCard`, `sessionEnd`.\n\n**Deep links** (`location.hash`, read at boot; for checks and screenshots):\n- Front end: `#title`, `#lobby`, `#howto`, `#scores`, `#about`, `#attract`.\n- Rooms: `#room=2-3`, `#boss=1|2|3`, `#greenroom`, `#lightning`.\n- Scenes: `#recap=2`, `#continue`, `#gameover`, `#initials`, `#ending=a|b`, `#credits`.\n- Tools: `#dev` (room 1-1 with the panel open), `#card` (a session-card preview with fake players).\n- Options, appended with `&`: `seed=N`, `players=N`, `bot=1`.\n\n```js\ngame.debug = {\n  snapshot() { return { scene, mode, ep, room, phase, wave, alive, byKind, bullets, pickups, R, X, keys,\n    players: [{ slot, x, y, hp, lives, cash, wpn, state }], boss: boss && { kind, phase, parts }, errors: game.errors.length }; },\n  warp(target, opts), spawn(kind, n, where), give(kind, slot = 0), cheat(name, on), tune(key, v), resetTunes(),\n  bot(slot, opts), addBot(look), killAll(), clearRoom(), chooseExit(dir), teleport(slot, x, y),\n  netFake(hub) /* inject a fake AIHub before connect */, seed(n), stateHash() };\ngame.stateHash = () => game.debug.stateHash();   // E.session replays stop at the first frame that differs\n```\n\n### 15.5 Engine patches (each marked in the source and listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`; they feed the Engine Feedback)\n\n| # | Patch | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1 | `E.HOOKS = HOOKS;` just after `HOOKS` is declared | The game must switch every player's input preset each step, before the Shell reads navigation: arrows aim in play and navigate in menus. |\n| P2 | The standard `glyphs` setting defaults to `'playstation'`, offers PlayStation or Xbox, and its `apply` sets `glyphs` on every `game.players` input | TASK.md wants PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option. The stock default is `'auto'`, and only P1 is updated. |\n| P3 | `E.Arcade.enter(score, mode, done, input)` reads the given input (default `game.input`); `continue()` takes a press from any `game.players` input | In co-op each player enters their own initials, and anyone may continue. |\n| P4 | `get bus() { this._init(); return this._sfxBus; }` on the audio object | Babble voices must pass through the effects bus to obey volume and mute. |\n\nPatch only what the game can't do itself: CSS overrides and our own UI need no patch. If the build needs another patch,\nadd it to this table with its reason.\n\n### 15.6 Coding rules\n\n- World units and `dt` everywhere. Pixels only when drawing.\n- Gameplay randomness only from `MT.rng.*`. `Math.random` only for cosmetics.\n- Logic never reads rig joints. Draw code reads only `rs`.\n- Gameplay emits events. Sound, text, crowd and banners come from the reaction table.\n- All text lives in `21-content-script.js`; every tunable lives in `MT.TDEF`.\n- Draw only with engine primitives: no `ctx.arc`, `fillText` or gradients on the game buffer.\n- No `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt` or `window.open`. Network only through `MT.hub`.\n- Headless-safe: guard every DOM and audio access (`game.headless`, `game.audio.ctx`).\n\n### 15.7 Building out the rest of the game later (the designer workflow)\n\nThe demo is a vertical slice. More content should take **data, not code**. Make these true, and test them:\n- **A new room** is one entry in `MT.ROOMS`: its waves, places, items, key rule and hazards, in the §5.7 format.\n  `#room=<id>` and dev WARP load it at once; RESTART ROOM reloads it after an edit; the `room-sweep` suite picks it up\n  automatically.\n- **A new episode** is one entry in `MT.EPISODES`: a 3×3 map, 6 rooms and a boss id, a set id, a song id, a stunt kind,\n  a prize table and an intro line. The episode select, the map card, the recap and the sweeps read it with no code\n  changes.\n- **A new enemy or weapon** is one registry entry (`MT.ENEMIES`, `MT.WEAPONS`) with its data and behaviour functions.\n  Spawn and give lists in dev mode read the registries, so a new entry shows there at once.\n- **A new line, prize or sponsor** is text in `MT.TEXT`, or a row in a prize table.\n- **Balance** is the dev sliders plus EXPORT TUNING, pasted into `MT.TDEF`.\n- **A new boss** reuses the framework's part, phase and pattern helpers (§5.9). It needs only its painter, `toNet` and\n  `fromNet`.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Build and packaging\n\n`npm run build` (`node tools/build.mjs`):\n1. **Concatenate** `src/*.js` in name order into `build/game.js`: one IIFE, opened in `00-core` and closed in\n   `90-main`. Inline `src/about.json` as `MT.ABOUT`, and stamp `MT.BUILD` with the ISO time.\n2. **Check:** `node --check build/game.js`.\n3. **Dev page:** write `build/dev.html`, which loads `../engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `../assets/mash-props.js` and `game.js`\n   as separate scripts, for fast iteration.\n4. **The deliverable:** write `../MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<ts>.html`.\n   - `<head>`, in this order:\n     1. `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n     2. viewport\n     3. `<title>MASH TV</title>`\n     4. the hub meta tags (§14.2)\n     5. `<style>`: the page, plus the 10-foot Shell CSS (§11.5)\n     6. **session.js verbatim, as the first `<script>`**\n   - `<body>`, in this order:\n     1. The boot splash markup (copy the template's `e3-boot` block, titled MASH TV).\n     2. `<canvas id=\"screen\">`.\n     3. Then three `<script>`s: the engine, the asset set, the game.\n5. **The exit interview:** write `../exit-interview.json` from `src/about.json` (§20). Check it with `JSON.parse` and a\n   check against the shape in TASK.md.\n\n- **Set the name once.** The model id and timestamp go in one constant in `tools/build.mjs`:\n  `OUT = 'MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<ts>'`, from your TASK.md.\n- **No network:** the deliverable must open from `file://` and make no network requests. `tools/check.mjs` lists any\n  external URLs.\n- **Size:** about 1.3 MB of engine plus the game. No minifying is needed. Never minify `session.js`.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Testing\n\n### 17.1 The one command\n\n`npm test` runs `node tools/test.mjs [--only suiteA,suiteB] [--no-browser]`.\n- It rebuilds first, runs every suite, and prints only failing checks (`suite › check: detail`) plus one line, like\n  `MASH TV tests: 412 passed, 0 failed (2m 41s)`. It exits with 1 on any failure.\n- **Headless suites:**\n  - Each loads the engine and `build/game.js` into a fresh `vm` context, with stand-ins for `window`,\n    `addEventListener`, `location.hash`, `matchMedia` and `navigator`, the way `kits/test.mjs` does.\n  - They drive the game with `game.advance`, `input.tap/hold/press/feed` and `game.debug`.\n  - `MT.cfg.skipRigs = true` keeps them fast. The animation checks are the exception.\n- **Browser suites** use Playwright's Chromium: it's already in `~/.cache/ms-playwright` on this machine; otherwise run\n  `npx playwright install chromium`.\n- **While iterating,** run only the suites you touched. At every milestone and at the end, run everything.\n\n### 17.2 Suites: what each checks\n\n| Suite | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| `core` | Tuning: defaults, set, reset, persistence. RNG streams are deterministic. The event bus. Arena geometry: door cells, interior bounds, cell↔world. |\n| `input` | **Presets:** in play, ArrowUp fires up; paused, ArrowDown moves the Shell's focus; resumed, ArrowUp fires again. **Intent:** the pad stick aims and fires; the mouse aims at `mouseGround`; arrows give 8 ways; R2 fires along the last aim; aim assist is pad-only. **Glyphs:** `lastDevice = gamepad` → ✕/L2/R1 icons, `xbox` → A/LT/RB; keyboard → Space/E/Q; mouse → Left click. |\n| `contestant` | Speed and walls. Slide distance, i-frames and cooldown. A hit costs 1 HP with invulnerability and knockback. Death → a life lost → the cable drop with invulnerability and at least 1 bomb. Out of lives → Start continues at half cash. The showboat formula, and its cancel on a hit. A Showstopper clears foe bullets, damages everything and uses stock. A drop-in adds a contestant. |\n| `weapons` | For each weapon and add-on: the pickup equips it; fire rate within ±5 %; damage; the spread's angles; the rocket splash; the popcorn lands and throws kernels; the laser pierces 3 goons in a line; records orbit, damage and block bullets; the shield blocks damage; sneakers give ×1.45; expiry goes back to the blaster; the same primary refreshes. |\n| `economy` | Cash × multiplier goes to the collector. Prizes don't repeat. Keys are shared. Drop caps. Expiry and blinking. The magnet after a clear. Crates break and drop. Extra lives at the thresholds. |\n| `ratings` | Gains, decay and losses; the tiers; **Prime Time** at 100 (12 s at ×5, 3 thrown capsules, back to 60, the cooldown); the **stunt** after 10 s under 5, once per room. |\n| `enemies` | For each kind (+ King, crate, lock): spawns from its door, the audience or a drop, and enters the interior; behaves as §5.8 says (one assert per behaviour: the goon closes in, the popper's 8 shards, the mime's telegraph then blast, the disco bounce and pattern, the big disco's split, the heckler's range and lob ring, the tank's telegraph then shell, snake segments shot off, the brute's charge then wall stun and ×2 damage); dies; drops; emits its confetti event; **every attack's wind-up is ≥ 0.3 s** before damage. |\n| `rooms` | The wave triggers (`at`, `left`, both) on a synthetic room. Scaling by players, difficulty and slider. Door timings. CLEAR ignores mimes, crates and locks. The clear bonus and par. Only listed exits open. An exit loads the right room with the opposite entry. HURRY UP after 25 s. Each key rule (`mimes`, `survive`, `crate`, `carrier`, `vault`, `final`) yields exactly one key. |\n| `room-sweep` | **Every room:** 18 wave rooms, 3 bosses, the Green Room, and the 4 Lightning rooms. Warp in; 2 god-mode bots play to CLEAR within 240 s of game time; no errors; the waves spawned match the data; every listed kind appeared. |\n| `bosses` | For each boss: intro, then the phases in order at their HP thresholds. Armor (Mashy's body tinks while an arm lives). Weak points take ×2. Every pattern fires at least once (every wheel segment via forced stops). Commercial Break: Lance is gone, then returns. The robot reveal leads to The Network, and THE EYE unlocks after 8 TVs. Death gives the reward shower, then the recap. |\n| `playthrough` | **The whole game, scripted.** 2 god-mode bots (damage ×3 for speed) take the left path in each episode: 1-1→1-2→1-4→1-5→1-B, 2-1→2-2→2-4→2-5→2-B, 3-1→3-2→3-4→3-5→3-B. Then keys ≥ 5, the Green Room, the statuette, Ending B, initials typed through input, Top Earners showing them, and back to the title. Asserts:<br>• Every enemy kind killed; every weapon and add-on picked up and used; both hazards met.<br>• Prime Time at least once; a Showstopper and a showboat used.<br>• 3 recaps with prizes; the robot reveal ran; scenes in order; 0 errors.<br>Then a **second run:** bots without cheats on ROOKIE in 1-1 until every life is gone → CONTINUE → let it run out → Game Over → initials. |\n| `ui` | Each title-menu item opens its screen and goes back. The lobby: join with `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })`, change looks, ready. Difficulty is applied. How to Play turns pages. Episodes lock and unlock. About has all four required sections, with no empty values. Mouse hover moves the focus and a click activates (`input.feed`). Every prompt bar uses the last device's glyphs. The pause menu has Dev mode, Episode map and How to play. |\n| `dev` | It opens from the pause item. FREEZE stops world time. Each slider changes `MT.T` and works live (`playerSpeed` ×2 doubles the distance in 1 s; `enemyHp` changes new goons). Per-row reset and RESET ALL restore defaults. Cheats work: god, one-hit, freeze, noclip. Every warp target loads. Spawning spawns. The DEV badge shows and blocks the high-score table. It can't be reached in sessions or the attract mode. |\n| `attract` | 10 s idle on the title → `attract`. All 10 reels play in order. The bots never die. Any input (a key, a mouse move over 8 px, a fake gamepad button, a touch) returns to the title within one step. Input resets the idle clock. It runs with the touch-only flag set. |\n| `touch` | Touch-only: the notice shows; SETTINGS opens settings; no touch buttons are registered; the attract mode runs; a key press or a gamepad connecting lifts the notice. |\n| `audio` | Headless: music, sound and babble calls never throw. `babble.say` durations scale with the text. Every scene sets a song. Every reaction id resolves to a real line or sound. |\n| `net` | A loopback through `tools/fakehub.mjs`: N `vm` contexts with an AIHub-compatible fake. It has a shared clock driven by the test loop, latency 150 ± 50 ms, 5 % loss, a limiter at 20/s with bursts of 40, a 16 KB cap, and reliable-ordered versus unreliable delivery.<br>It runs host+bot with 2 clients+bots, then server + 2 clients. Asserts:<br>• connect and ready with no input; the card's answers (confirm → `ready`, △ → `more`); the countdown, then play at `time() >= 0`.<br>• Traffic both ways, at ≤ 20 msg/s, with max size ≤ 12 KB.<br>• Clients' enemy counts within 10 % of the host's, and 90 % of matched positions within 24 units.<br>• Client bots earn cash; a claim resolves once; a client's `hurt` shows in the host's snapshot.<br>• Room changes reach every copy within 1 s.<br>• Mashy's death → `finish` once, with valid standings and `won: true`; time-up finishes within 1 s.<br>• `leave` removes the contestant; server mode runs and sends.<br>• The **session overlay opened on the host doesn't stop snapshots**.<br>• `end` stops input and audio.<br>• `alert/confirm/prompt/open/WebSocket/RTCPeerConnection/fetch` are stubbed to fail the test. |\n| `hub` (browser) | `tools/hub-check.mjs`. Copy the deliverable and `vendor/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}` into a serve directory **inside the dev folder**, and serve it on `127.0.0.1:0`, keeping its PID. Check that the page served is MASH TV. Then run the harness 4 times:<br>• `?game=<file>&players=2&auto=1`<br>• `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`<br>• the same with `&scenario=host-leave`<br>• `&players=2&scenario=server&auto=1`<br>Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'`, require `window.__hubCheck.pass`, and print any failed checks. Kill the server by PID. |\n| `browser` | Playwright on the deliverable over `file://`: no console or page errors, no external requests. Every deep link draws a real frame, with pixel variance above a threshold: `#title`, `#lobby`, `#room=1-1`, `#room=2-2`, `#room=3-2`, `#boss=1..3`, `#greenroom`, `#recap=1`, `#ending=b`, `#about`, `#dev`, `#card`. `tools/check.mjs` passes at 1280×720 and 1920×1080. Touch emulation (`390x844@3`) shows the notice. Keyboard focus and Enter go from the title to the lobby. Screenshots go to `shots/`. |\n| `proof` | Determinism: seed 7, room 1-1, 2 bots for 30 s → the same `stateHash` twice. |\n\n### 17.3 What only eyes and ears can judge\n\nThese need screenshots or listening; scripts can't judge them:\n- **Look at `shots/`:** the look target for every set (§9.1); text readable at 1080p (try the screenshot at 25 % size);\n  the HUD clear of the corner buttons; boss silhouettes readable; confetti never hiding bullets.\n- **Act on every look note** from `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Filmstrips** (`node tools/filmstrip.mjs <page>#room=1-1 --steps \"…\" --seed 1`) for every attack, pose and boss\n  telegraph: a wind-up, a strike and a settle, with no jerks.\n- **Listen once** in a real browser to each song, the voices and each group of sounds.\n\n---\n\n## 18. Build order and milestones\n\nEach milestone ends with a playable build and its tests green. Build the riskiest systems early, the netcode especially.\n\n| # | Milestone | Deliverables | Gate (must pass) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M0 | **Setup** | dev folder; engine and hub fetched; `package.json`; `build.mjs` producing the single HTML; `test.mjs` runner; patches P1–P4 and `PATCHES.md`; a bare title scene | `core`; the browser smoke (it loads with no errors) |\n| M1 | **Core arena loop** | 23×15 arena with doors and camera fit; the contestant (every input path, blaster, slide, HP, lives, respawn); goon; bullet pools; the damage pipeline; kill FX; basic HUD; presets and the hook; glyph prompts | `input`, `contestant` (partial), `weapons` (blaster). A person can play 1-1's first waves. |\n| M2 | **Netcode prototype** (de-risk) | the World's authoritative and mirror modes; the render-state contract; FakeHub; snapshots; interpolation; client prediction; claims and hurts; room messages. Goons only, one room. | `net` core: traffic, rates, sizes, convergence, kill credit |\n| M3 | **Room flow + Episode 1** | the wave runner; door spawns, exits and signs; the phase machine; map and episode cards; ratings with Prime Time and stunts; pickups and economy; all weapons and add-ons; clapbot, popper, mime, crates, key rules; rooms 1-1 to 1-6; local co-op join | `rooms`, `weapons`, `economy`, `ratings`, `room-sweep` (Ep1) |\n| M4 | **Bosses + end of run** | the boss framework and Mashy; Prize Recap; continue; game over; initials; Top Earners (E.Arcade + P3) | `bosses` (Mashy), an Ep1 bot playthrough, the end flow in `ui` |\n| M5 | **Episodes 2 and 3** | disco, heckler (+King), tank, snake, brute; conveyors and pyro; rooms 2-x and 3-x; the Wheel; Starmaker → Network; Green Room; both endings; the cold open and every cutscene | `enemies`, `bosses`, `room-sweep` (all), `playthrough` |\n| M6 | **AI Hub** | the session-aware game; tags; the client as the first script; the card; the Lightning Round; the session overlay; finish, standings and awards; leave, end, host-left and server | `net` (full), `hub` (all 4 harness runs PASS) |\n| M7 | **Presentation** | sets and outside dressing; dressed characters; final enemy and boss painters; animations (mime box, brute stun…); lights per set (+GPU); effects; logo; final HUD; banners; jumbotron and lower thirds | `browser`; screenshots reviewed against §9; filmstrips; look notes clean |\n| M8 | **Audio** | songs and layers; sounds; Babble voices; crowd; the full reaction table | `audio`; one listening pass |\n| M9 | **Front end and UX** | every screen in §11.3; UI kit polish; 10-foot CSS; settings; touch-only; the attract mode with all reels; the full dev mode | `ui`, `dev`, `attract`, `touch` |\n| M10 | **Balance, polish, ship** | tuning with the dev sliders to the targets below; the About & Credits content; the exit interview; final `npm test` all green; the deliverable and `exit-interview.json` in the root; the chat reply | everything |\n\n**Balance targets for M10** (use the bot without cheats as a stand-in player, then play it yourself):\n- Rooms: one bot on CONTESTANT clears each Episode 1 room in 50–90 s, with at most 2 deaths per room.\n- A full run lasts 20–25 minutes. A session round with 2 bots lasts about 5 minutes.\n- Prime Time comes at least once per episode.\n- No foe bullet is faster than 130 u/s, and no attack lands with less than a 0.3 s telegraph.\n- The busiest snapshot (room 3-5, wave 5, 4 players) stays under 12 KB.\n\n---\n\n## 19. Risks and how to de-risk them\n\n| Risk | Why it's risky | De-risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shared-world netcode | The most complex part, and the hub's check is strict (latency, loss, host leaving, a server copy). | The M2 prototype before the content; a strict authority split; compact snapshots; the rate budget; run the real harness as soon as M6 starts; the ghost fallback documented (§14.5). |\n| The engine's pause in sessions | A paused engine skips `update`, so the host's world would freeze for everyone. | In sessions: no pausable scenes, no auto-pause, a non-pausing overlay; the `net` test opens it on the host. |\n| Arrows aim, but menus need them | The stock presets put arrows on the moves; the Shell navigates by the `up`/`down` actions. | Two presets and the P1 hook; the `input` suite. |\n| The font has no PlayStation symbols | ✕ ○ □ △ aren't in the 5×7 font. | The glyph icon module (§4.4), tested through action → icon. |\n| Hordes of HD rigs | Cost per frame, and test speed. | `skipRigs` in tests; draw goon and clapbot crowds with `r.actor(…, { every: 2, key, outline: false })`. Performance is out of scope, but the game must stay playable. |\n| Three multi-phase bosses | Many patterns and states. | One framework; patterns as coroutines; forced phases and segments in dev and tests; one test per boss. |\n| Engine Shell screens at 10 feet | The stock CSS uses 11–14 px text. | The CSS override (§11.5) and 1080p screenshots. |\n| The harness pokes keys with `code` = the key (e.g. `'a'`) | Unknown codes are ignored, and Enter must still answer Ready. | Handle Enter through both the action and a raw keydown backstop on the card. |\n| Determinism | Random draws in the simulation. | Seeded `MT.rng`; `Math.random` for cosmetics only; the `proof` suite. |\n| Scope | 18 rooms, 3 bosses, 9 enemies. | Rooms are data; enemies and bosses reuse spawners (the wheel's segments call the enemy spawners); every milestone keeps a playable build. |\n| A full `/tmp` on this machine | During planning, the tmpfs at `/tmp` was 100 % full. | Keep clones, caches and serve directories inside the dev folder. |\n| False touch-only detection | Laptops with touch screens. | Require *no* fine pointer *and* no key or pad input yet; lift the notice on the first key or pad. |\n\n---\n\n## 20. Finish checklist\n\n- [ ] Everything in §4–§14 is built. `grep -rniE \"todo|tbd|fixme|placeholder|lorem|coming soon\" src/` finds nothing.\n- [ ] `npm test` is all green, including `hub` (4/4 harness PASS) and `browser`.\n- [ ] `MashTV-SmashTV-<model>-<ts>.html` is in the root folder and opens from `file://`. `session.js` is its first\n      script and is unmodified.\n- [ ] **About & Credits** in the game, generated from `src/about.json`, has real values:\n  - **Source Information:**\n    - Date: from your TASK.md.\n    - Source: \"Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\".\n    - Plan: \"PLAN.md\".\n    - Genre and Perspective: \"Twin-stick arena shooter; fixed three-quarter overhead (top-down option)\".\n    - Tech: the engine version and patches, the hub client version, the Node and Playwright versions you used.\n    - Art Direction, Animation, Audio.\n    - Online: \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\".\n    - Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage.\n    - Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n  - Then your answers to the Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback questions.\n- [ ] `exit-interview.json` sits beside the HTML, generated from the same `about.json`. It parses as JSON and has\n      exactly the shape TASK.md gives. Suggested `aiHub` values:\n  - `name`: \"MASH TV\"\n  - `tagline`: \"Mash 'em! Cash 'em! Survive the show.\"\n  - `description`: one or two sentences, e.g. \"A 1–4 player twin-stick arena shooter inside the deadliest game show on\n    TV: blast through studio rooms of goons and robots, grab cash and absurd prizes, keep the ratings up and survive to\n    the Season Finale.\"\n  - `type`: \"Game\"\n  - `genre`: \"Shooter\"\n  - `source`: \"Smash TV\"\n  - `series`: \"\"\n  - `players`: \"1–4\"\n  - `controls`: \"WASD/left stick move · mouse/right stick aim & fire · Space/L2 slide · E/R1 Showstopper · Q/L1 Showboat\"\n  - `tags`: [\"arcade\", \"co-op\", \"top-down\", \"pixel art\", \"comedy\", \"boss fights\"]\n- [ ] The chat reply has the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback, with the same\n      content as the About screen.\n- [ ] Every process you started has been stopped, by PID.\n\n---\n\n## Appendix A. Research behind this plan\n\n- **Engine** (my-3D2dge handoff v0.85.2, commit `4390a98`):\n  - Read `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), and the `AI_GUIDE.md` sections listed in §1, including the Twin-stick recipe.\n  - Read the `API-INDEX.md` sections on input, audio, standard features, the Shell and the arcade module, and the\n    template `game/`.\n  - Verified in the engine source: the input presets and arrow handling, the Shell's navigation and pause gating\n    (`_step` returns while paused), the pause-on-menu behaviour, player joining (`allowJoin`, `addPlayer`), the audio\n    buses, the glyph setting, and the arcade module's input use.\n  - Searched the asset store for studio and prize props (televisions, presents, sofas, fridges, cakes, speakers, lamps,\n    crates).\n- **AI Hub** (`lib/hub`): read `README.md` (the protocol), `retrofit.md`, the harness's automatic checks and timings\n  (`harness.html`, `round.js`: hello 10 s, countdown 3 s, grace 5 s), the bridge's card and answer handling, the\n  client's wrappers (storage, fullscreen, seeding), and the shared-world example `examples/arena.html`.\n- **Smash TV:** written from general knowledge; no web research. Every name, character, line, level, enemy, boss,\n  prize and piece of music in this plan is original.","numLines":336,"startLine":1681,"totalLines":2017}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PVKmf7MVz4eYDBKe5cqTk9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
