{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghuyzuBg1553F9YD9qav","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game now includes AI Hub shared-world play and the Lightning Round, and all four hub harness scenarios pass. The completion criterion is still not fully met, though: several features are only partly done, so I can't say every TASK feature is finished.\n\n**Deliverables** (in the work folder root)\n- `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050305.html`: the single bundled file, with the engine, the game and the AI Hub client `session.js` (pasted unmodified, first script in head).\n- `exit-interview.json`: parses, and answers every field.\n- No TODO, TBD, FIXME, placeholder or \"coming soon\" text in `src/`.\n\n**Done this round**\n- **AI Hub shared-world:** the host runs the world, or a server copy runs it with no player. Other copies send their intents and mirror the host's 15-per-second snapshots, with room changes sent reliably. The host finishes once with standings, scores, stats and awards.\n- **Harness results** (served from `serve/` on 127.0.0.1): two players, four players with 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, host leaving (the round is voided as expected), and a server copy. All pass.\n- **Lightning Round:** room 1-1 waves, 1-2, 2-3, then Mashy. Each has one exit, N, to the next. Lives are unlimited, respawns cost $2,500, and keys pay $10,000.\n- **Session card and overlay:** the card shows the line-up, the ready answers, the time left and the controls. Esc opens volume, music and button-symbol options and never pauses.\n- **Outside dressing:** bleachers, audience, jumbotron and cranes around the arena.\n- **Logo:** the studio floor samples the engine's title font, rasterized from an offscreen canvas in the browser. I could not check this in the browser myself, so treat it as unverified.\n- **Recap:** prizes appear one by one with Penny's line, totals count up, and an episode MVP is named.\n- **Attract:** the forced moments are in: the key at 10 s, Mashy's arms off, the wheel's forced segments, and the Starmaker's cut to the Network. The card reels speak their lines.\n- **Test suites:** 119 checks pass (91 fast and 28 heavy). The hub runs are separate and are not part of `npm test`.\n\n**Still not done or only partly done against the TASK**\n- **Endings A and B and the Green Room:** the endings are text cards over the studio backdrop, with no staged poses or cutaway. The Green Room runs its bonus with cash and paparazzi, but its presentation is basic.\n- **Animations:** the mime's \"trapped in a box\" pose, and the brute's stun wobble with orbiting stars, are not implemented. Other animation uses the engine's rigs and simple custom painters.\n- **Audio:** the babble voices and the music are coded and checked by duration and by the engine's own playback, but I have not listened to them.\n- **Netcode simplifications:** clients show the host's snapshot for other players, with no interpolation. Pickups are decided only by the host.\n- **Hub check status:** the hub harness was run by hand from `tools/hub-check.py`. The results are not in `npm test`.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:05\n- Source: Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and Perspective: twin-stick arena shooter, 1–4 players local and online; fixed three-quarter studio camera, with an overhead option\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with patches P1, P3 and P4; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, unmodified; Node v24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0 checks; single HTML, no libraries\n- Art Direction: retro-future TV studio, neon and chrome, with the engine's PS1 look and HD rigs\n- Animation: engine rigs and poses; custom painters for the disco ball, dolly tank, cable snake and bosses\n- Audio: all music, sound effects and voices synthesized in code; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\n- Testing: headless suites in a VM; AI Hub harness runs; real-browser checks with key presses\n- Research: engine docs and source; AI Hub README and client; Smash TV written from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser Chromium (headless)\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 10 real game problems were found and fixed in the earlier rounds, with about 5 game-breaking: walkers that never came in, so a room could not clear; Lance's TV wall placed above the arena; the continue countdown that never ticked; the Green Room that never started; and the attract scene that was never registered. This round, the netcode and the attract fix added a few more bugs, all found by the suites or the harness and fixed.\n- The bot playthrough of the whole left path was the most useful test. It runs the whole flow and finds stalls that per-function checks miss.\n- The touch check is the least useful: it sets globals and checks one function.\n- The hub harness is strict and specific, and its checks are what showed the protocol gaps.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- I liked the arcade module (scores, initials, continue) and the input presets. `game.script` with `until` made the run flow readable.\n- I was stuck on: the input hook and the arcade's per-player input were private, so I patched them (P1, P3). `audio.layer` warns on missing tracks, which I guarded. Overlay units are buffer pixels, about a third of a 1080p screen, which I learned by trial.\n- The most important parts I built were the room runner, the enemy archetypes, the boss framework, the PlayStation and Xbox glyph icons, the reaction table, the Animalese voices, and the AI Hub shared-world netcode.\n- The hardest parts were the overlay units and having no headless draw test.\n- Engine changes are P1, P3 and P4, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n- One change I'd make: export the input hook and the arcade's per-player input by default, so no patch is needed.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- I liked the detailed room data and the clear exit-interview shape.\n- It was confusing which parts were required for a first delivery and which were stretch goals, such as the AI Hub netcode and the full test-suite breadth.\n- The hardest parts were scoping the AI Hub netcode in one session and getting the hub harness to pass under latency and loss.\n- I'd split the plan into a first playable milestone and stretch milestones, and state which suites are required for delivery."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":704782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":725},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eec69157-3f75-40b4-be93-e6ea9b3a321a","uuid":"12439165-fdf6-477b-be68-9a6283b7837c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:48:37.582Z","request_id":"req_011CghuyzX7bstZTjzTDMr6b"}
{"duration_api_ms":2053614,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"eec69157-3f75-40b4-be93-e6ea9b3a321a","total_cost_usd":7.414234634999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":438,"cache_creation_input_tokens":689698,"cache_read_input_tokens":108788647,"output_tokens":441067,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":165658},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":689698,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2289,"cache_read_input_tokens":704782,"cache_creation_input_tokens":725,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":725},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":442,"outputTokens":441286,"cacheReadInputTokens":108789519,"cacheCreationInputTokens":1115271,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":7.414234634999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":165658,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":425573,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":689698,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":223,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The game now includes AI Hub shared-world play and the Lightning Round, and all four hub harness scenarios pass. The completion criterion is still not fully met, though: several features are only partly done, so I can't say every TASK feature is finished.\n\n**Deliverables** (in the work folder root)\n- `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050305.html`: the single bundled file, with the engine, the game and the AI Hub client `session.js` (pasted unmodified, first script in head).\n- `exit-interview.json`: parses, and answers every field.\n- No TODO, TBD, FIXME, placeholder or \"coming soon\" text in `src/`.\n\n**Done this round**\n- **AI Hub shared-world:** the host runs the world, or a server copy runs it with no player. Other copies send their intents and mirror the host's 15-per-second snapshots, with room changes sent reliably. The host finishes once with standings, scores, stats and awards.\n- **Harness results** (served from `serve/` on 127.0.0.1): two players, four players with 150 ms latency, jitter and 5% loss, host leaving (the round is voided as expected), and a server copy. All pass.\n- **Lightning Round:** room 1-1 waves, 1-2, 2-3, then Mashy. Each has one exit, N, to the next. Lives are unlimited, respawns cost $2,500, and keys pay $10,000.\n- **Session card and overlay:** the card shows the line-up, the ready answers, the time left and the controls. Esc opens volume, music and button-symbol options and never pauses.\n- **Outside dressing:** bleachers, audience, jumbotron and cranes around the arena.\n- **Logo:** the studio floor samples the engine's title font, rasterized from an offscreen canvas in the browser. I could not check this in the browser myself, so treat it as unverified.\n- **Recap:** prizes appear one by one with Penny's line, totals count up, and an episode MVP is named.\n- **Attract:** the forced moments are in: the key at 10 s, Mashy's arms off, the wheel's forced segments, and the Starmaker's cut to the Network. The card reels speak their lines.\n- **Test suites:** 119 checks pass (91 fast and 28 heavy). The hub runs are separate and are not part of `npm test`.\n\n**Still not done or only partly done against the TASK**\n- **Endings A and B and the Green Room:** the endings are text cards over the studio backdrop, with no staged poses or cutaway. The Green Room runs its bonus with cash and paparazzi, but its presentation is basic.\n- **Animations:** the mime's \"trapped in a box\" pose, and the brute's stun wobble with orbiting stars, are not implemented. Other animation uses the engine's rigs and simple custom painters.\n- **Audio:** the babble voices and the music are coded and checked by duration and by the engine's own playback, but I have not listened to them.\n- **Netcode simplifications:** clients show the host's snapshot for other players, with no interpolation. Pickups are decided only by the host.\n- **Hub check status:** the hub harness was run by hand from `tools/hub-check.py`. The results are not in `npm test`.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:05\n- Source: Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and Perspective: twin-stick arena shooter, 1–4 players local and online; fixed three-quarter studio camera, with an overhead option\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with patches P1, P3 and P4; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, unmodified; Node v24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0 checks; single HTML, no libraries\n- Art Direction: retro-future TV studio, neon and chrome, with the engine's PS1 look and HD rigs\n- Animation: engine rigs and poses; custom painters for the disco ball, dolly tank, cable snake and bosses\n- Audio: all music, sound effects and voices synthesized in code; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\n- Testing: headless suites in a VM; AI Hub harness runs; real-browser checks with key presses\n- Research: engine docs and source; AI Hub README and client; Smash TV written from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser Chromium (headless)\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 10 real game problems were found and fixed in the earlier rounds, with about 5 game-breaking: walkers that never came in, so a room could not clear; Lance's TV wall placed above the arena; the continue countdown that never ticked; the Green Room that never started; and the attract scene that was never registered. This round, the netcode and the attract fix added a few more bugs, all found by the suites or the harness and fixed.\n- The bot playthrough of the whole left path was the most useful test. It runs the whole flow and finds stalls that per-function checks miss.\n- The touch check is the least useful: it sets globals and checks one function.\n- The hub harness is strict and specific, and its checks are what showed the protocol gaps.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- I liked the arcade module (scores, initials, continue) and the input presets. `game.script` with `until` made the run flow readable.\n- I was stuck on: the input hook and the arcade's per-player input were private, so I patched them (P1, P3). `audio.layer` warns on missing tracks, which I guarded. Overlay units are buffer pixels, about a third of a 1080p screen, which I learned by trial.\n- The most important parts I built were the room runner, the enemy archetypes, the boss framework, the PlayStation and Xbox glyph icons, the reaction table, the Animalese voices, and the AI Hub shared-world netcode.\n- The hardest parts were the overlay units and having no headless draw test.\n- Engine changes are P1, P3 and P4, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n- One change I'd make: export the input hook and the arcade's per-player input by default, so no patch is needed.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- I liked the detailed room data and the clear exit-interview shape.\n- It was confusing which parts were required for a first delivery and which were stretch goals, such as the AI Hub netcode and the full test-suite breadth.\n- The hardest parts were scoping the AI Hub netcode in one session and getting the hub harness to pass under latency and loss.\n- I'd split the plan into a first playable milestone and stretch milestones, and state which suites are required for delivery.","ttft_ms":1780,"type":"result","duration_ms":2731650,"uuid":"153bfc34-1698-4c1f-9dbc-082dda64234c","ttft_stream_ms":1338,"time_to_request_ms":87,"first_content_frame_ms":1338,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":442,"outputTokens":441286,"cacheReadInputTokens":108789519,"cacheCreationInputTokens":1115271,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":7.414234634999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":165658,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":425573,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":689698,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}}}
