{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvZdne2tCiTUPfdwzqP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The three gaps from the last review are now addressed in code: the staged endings, the mime and brute animations, and the Green Room light. Every feature in TASK.md is not yet fully implemented to the plan's standard, so the completion criterion is still not met.\n\n**What changed this round**\n- **Endings:** Ending A and Ending B are now staged in the play scene instead of shown as text cards. The contestants pose in a row under the spotlight. In Ending A the spare Lance robot reboots behind the curtain. In Ending B the contestants point at a red OFF switch while the lights go out one by one. I checked both in headless Chromium, and each renders.\n- **Mime:** a glass box surrounds it and shakes while its blast telegraphs. It uses the block pose.\n- **Brute:** a stunned brute wobbles and has stars orbiting its head.\n- **Green Room:** the statuette has a light pool and a ring on the floor.\n- **Fixes:** the room hint showed unfilled `{MOVE}` and `{AIM}` placeholders, which now read as plain text. A stale line also stayed on screen when an ending began.\n\n**Tests:** all 119 checks pass (91 fast and 28 heavy). The staged endings were also run headless through a full Green Room path with no errors. The AI Hub harness results from the last round still stand; this round didn't touch the netcode.\n\n**Still not fully done against the TASK**\n- **Animation:** the mime's hands don't flatten against the glass. It uses the block pose and a shaking box instead. Other plan animations are not done either, including the Starmaker jets and the Wheel rolling.\n- **Endings and Green Room:** the endings use simple staging, with no sound-synced beats. The Green Room is a 40-second bonus with cash, paparazzi and a statuette, not the fuller room the plan describes.\n- **Listening:** I have not heard the music, babble voices or sound effects, so they are unverified by ear.\n- **Floor logo:** it is sampled from an offscreen canvas in the browser. I could not confirm in a browser that it draws, so treat it as unverified.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:05\n- Source: Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and Perspective: twin-stick arena shooter, 1–4 players local and online; fixed three-quarter studio camera, with an overhead option\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with patches P1, P3 and P4; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, unmodified; Node v24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0; a single HTML file with no libraries\n- Art Direction: retro-future TV studio in neon and chrome, with the engine's PS1 look and HD rigs\n- Animation: engine rigs and poses; custom painters for the disco ball, dolly tank, cable snake and bosses; glass box and brute wobble added this round\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voices synthesized in code; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\n- Testing: headless suites in a VM; AI Hub harness runs; real-browser checks with key presses\n- Research: engine docs and source; AI Hub README and client; Smash TV written from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium (headless)\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Roughly 10 real game problems were found and fixed across the build, about 5 of them game-breaking: walkers that never came in, Lance's TV wall above the arena, the continue countdown that never ticked, the Green Room that never started, and the attract scene that was never registered. This round the bugs were a duplicate draw and a missing variable in the attract code. Both were caught by the checks and fixed.\n- The bot playthrough of the whole left path was most useful. It runs the whole flow and finds stalls that per-function checks miss.\n- The touch check is least useful: it sets globals and checks one function.\n- The hub harness is strict, and its checks are what showed the protocol gaps.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- I liked the arcade module and the input presets. `game.script` with `until` made the run flow readable.\n- I was stuck on the input hook and the arcade's per-player input. Both were private, so I patched them (P1, P3). `audio.layer` warns on missing tracks, which I guarded. Overlay units are buffer pixels, about a third of a 1080p screen, which I learned by trial.\n- The most important parts I built were the room runner, the enemy archetypes, the boss framework, the PlayStation and Xbox glyph icons, the reaction table, the Animalese voices, and the AI Hub shared-world netcode.\n- The hardest parts were the overlay units and having no headless draw test.\n- Engine changes are P1, P3 and P4, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n- I would export the input hook and the arcade's per-player input by default, so no patch is needed.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- I liked the detailed room data and the clear exit-interview shape.\n- It was confusing which parts were required for a first delivery and which were stretch goals, such as the AI Hub netcode and the full test-suite breadth.\n- The hardest parts were scoping the AI Hub netcode in one session and getting the hub harness to pass under latency and loss.\n- I would split the plan into a first playable milestone and stretch milestones, and state which suites are required for delivery.\n\n**Deliverables** (work folder root)\n- `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050305.html`: the single bundled file.\n- `exit-interview.json`: regenerated from `src/about.json` and parses."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":294,"cache_read_input_tokens":737494,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":294},"output_tokens":2,"se