{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvjUa2RRyPciUE7vp6t","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 119 checks pass on the final build, including the playthrough that the beat fix had broken.\n\n**What changed this round**\n- **Mime hands:** both hands are now set flat on the glass at chest height, sliding along it on a slow sine. This uses the engine's `solvePose` wrapper, as its guide describes.\n- **Wheel:** the wheel turns with its rolling speed.\n- **Starmaker:** two flickering hover jets under the podium.\n- **Endings:** sounds now fire at set times: a drumroll and a boot-up sting in Ending A, a click for each light in Ending B, and a boo and the final lose sting. They are timed, not synced to the music.\n- **Green Room:** two sofas, a cake table with a cake, and TVs on the wall, added to the existing floor, panelling, paparazzi and statuette light.\n\n**Still short of the plan**\n- The endings' beats are timed, not synced to music. The Green Room is simpler than the plan's full room, with no portraits or separate lounge areas.\n- The plan's GPU lighting for each set is not implemented.\n- I have not heard the music or voices.\n\nI can't claim every TASK feature is finished to the plan's standard. Whether the completion criterion is met is for the reviewer to judge against those gaps.\n\n**Deliverables** (work folder root)\n- `MashTV-SmashTV-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050305.html`: the single bundled file.\n- `exit-interview.json`: regenerated from `src/about.json`, and it parses.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:05\n- Source: Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and Perspective: twin-stick arena shooter, 1–4 players local and online; fixed three-quarter studio camera, with an overhead option\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with patches P1, P3 and P4; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, unmodified; Node v24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0; a single HTML file with no libraries\n- Art Direction: retro-future TV studio in neon and chrome, with the engine's PS1 look and HD rigs\n- Animation: engine rigs and poses; custom painters for the disco ball, dolly tank, cable snake and bosses; glass box, brute wobble, mime hands, wheel rolling and Starmaker jets this round\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voices synthesized in code; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\n- Testing: headless suites in a VM; AI Hub harness runs; real-browser checks with key presses\n- Research: engine docs and source; AI Hub README and client; Smash TV written from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium (headless)\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 10 real game problems were found and fixed across the build, about 5 of them game-breaking: walkers that never came in, Lance's TV wall above the arena, the continue countdown that never ticked, the Green Room that never started, and the attract scene that was never registered. This round the bugs were a duplicate draw, a missing variable, and a loop-scope error in the beats. The suites caught all three.\n- The bot playthrough of the whole left path was most useful. It runs the whole flow and finds stalls that per-function checks miss.\n- The touch check is least useful: it sets globals and checks one function.\n- The hub harness is strict, and its checks are what showed the protocol gaps.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- I liked the arcade module and the input presets. `game.script` with `until` made the run flow readable.\n- I was stuck on the input hook and the arcade's per-player input. Both were private, so I patched them (P1, P3). `audio.layer` warns on missing tracks, which I guarded. Overlay units are buffer pixels, about a third of a 1080p screen, which I learned by trial.\n- The most important parts I built were the room runner, the enemy archetypes, the boss framework, the PlayStation and Xbox glyph icons, the reaction table, the Animalese voices, and the AI Hub shared-world netcode.\n- The hardest parts were the overlay units and having no headless draw test.\n- Engine changes are P1, P3 and P4, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n- I would export the input hook and the arcade's per-player input by default, so no patch is needed.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- I liked the detailed room data and the clear exit-interview shape.\n- It was confusing which parts were required for a first delivery and which were stretch goals, such as the AI Hub netcode and the full test-suite breadth.\n- The hardest parts were scoping the AI Hub netcode in one session and getting the hub harness to pass under latency and loss.\n- I would split the plan into a first playable milestone and stretch milestones, and state which suites are required for delivery."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":750,"cache_read_input_tokens":752427,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":750},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eec69157-3f75-40b4-be93-e6ea9b3a321a","uuid":"b2e149dd-a746-495c-9a3b-725864a01ad4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:58:24.570Z","request_id":"req_011CghvjU9jY64bmeoGFMKY6"}
