{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvqHs1jEN5TwnKGix7h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't truthfully say every TASK feature is finished, so the completion criterion is not met. The bundled HTML and `exit-interview.json` are in the work folder root, and the four feedback sections are below. The test suite passes, 119 of 119.\n\n**Still not done or unverified**\n- **GPU lighting per set:** not implemented. The game uses the engine's canvas lighting, with each set's colours as slow spotlights. The engine's GPU path needs WebGPU, which I can't test here.\n- **Ending beats:** timed to each song's tempo from the stage's start, but not synced to the actual music playback.\n- **Green Room:** simpler than the plan's full room. It has sofas, a cake table, TVs, paparazzi and the statuette light, but not the plan's full layout or dressing.\n- **Audio:** I have not heard the music, voices or sound effects. Their correctness is unverified.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:03:05\n- Source: Smash TV (Williams Electronics arcade game, 1990)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and Perspective: twin-stick arena shooter, 1–4 players local and online; fixed three-quarter studio camera, with an overhead option\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 with patches P1, P3 and P4; AI Hub client aihub-session 1.4.0, unmodified; Node v24.22.1; Playwright 1.63.0; a single HTML file with no libraries\n- Art Direction: retro-future TV studio in neon and chrome, with the engine's PS1 look and HD rigs\n- Animation: engine rigs and poses; custom painters for the disco ball, dolly tank, cable snake and bosses; glass box, brute wobble, mime hands, wheel rolling and Starmaker jets\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voices synthesized in code; no audio files\n- Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 1–4 players; local co-op 1–4\n- Testing: headless suites in a VM; AI Hub harness runs; real-browser checks with key presses\n- Research: engine docs and source; AI Hub README and client; Smash TV written from general knowledge\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium (headless)\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- About 10 real game problems were found and fixed across the build, about 5 of them game-breaking: walkers that never came in, Lance's TV wall above the arena, the continue countdown that never ticked, the Green Room that never started, and the attract scene that was never registered. Later rounds caught a duplicate draw, a missing variable and a loop-scope error, all by the suites.\n- The bot playthrough of the whole left path was most useful, since it exercises the whole flow.\n- The touch check is least useful: it sets globals and checks one function.\n- The hub harness is strict, and its checks showed the protocol gaps.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- I liked the arcade module and the input presets. `game.script` with `until` made the run flow readable.\n- I was stuck on the input hook and the arcade's per-player input, both private, so I patched them (P1, P3). `audio.layer` warns on missing tracks, which I guarded. Overlay units are buffer pixels, about a third of a 1080p screen, which I learned by trial.\n- The most important parts I built were the room runner, enemy archetypes, boss framework, PlayStation and Xbox glyph icons, reaction table, Animalese voices, and the AI Hub shared-world netcode.\n- The hardest parts were the overlay units and having no headless draw test.\n- Engine changes are P1, P3 and P4, listed in `engine/PATCHES.md`.\n- I would export the input hook and the arcade's per-player input by default, so no patch is needed.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- I liked the detailed room data and the clear exit-interview shape.\n- It was confusing which parts were required for a first delivery and which were stretch goals, such as the AI Hub netcode and the full test-suite breadth.\n- The hardest parts were scoping the AI Hub netcode in one session and getting the hub harness to pass under latency and loss.\n- I would split the plan into a first playable milestone and stretch milestones, and state which suites are required for delivery."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":908,"cache_read_input_tokens":757826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":908},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eec69157-3f75-40b4-be93-e6ea9b3a321a","uuid":"5a977b17-1249-4c60-a5c6-5ea9ad471db9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:59:40.965Z","request_id":"req_011CghvqHThtXv49SiiKBTac"}
